WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:26.100
He’s a man known to history literally as
‘the Terrible’.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.330
Ivan IV was the first tsar of Russia, the
man who built the iconic St Basil’s Cathedral,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.450
and the ruler who defended Moscow from Tartar
invasion.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.660
In Russian history, he’s a towering figure,
behind perhaps only Peter the Great and Lenin

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.480
in how he shaped the nation.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.640
Yet how much do any of us really know about
him, beyond his mistranslated nickname?

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.510
Could he really be so bad as the crazed, wild-eyed
caricature of popular history?

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.510
Well, no.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:58.680
As it turns out, Ivan the Terrible was, if
anything, even more terrifying.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.200
Elevated to ruler of Russia when he was just
a boy, Ivan showed sociopathic tendencies

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.470
from the get-go.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.170
As a child, he was renowned for torturing
small animals.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.970
As an adult, he had his opponents torn apart
by wild dogs.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.960
A bloodthirsty king who killed his own son
and created a feared secret police, this is

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.280
the story of Russia’s sixteenth century
Stalin.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.490
“An evil act will spawn an evil son”

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:32.080
On August 25, 1530, Vasili III gazed out the
window of his palace as a terrific storm battered

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.080
Moscow.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.010
It was twenty years since the Grand Prince
had come to the throne, twenty long years

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.210
in which he’d tried fruitlessly for an heir.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.400
Now, nearing the end of his life, his desperation
had got the better of him.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.370
He’d divorced his first wife and remarried,
scandalizing Moscow.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:52.100
One religious man had even prophesized that
this evil act would produce an evil heir,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.990
one who would terrorize the country.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.950
We can only wonder if Vasili III was reminded
of that prophecy as he looked out the window

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.610
at the storm wracking Moscow the night his
son was born.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:08.200
The Russia Ivan the Terrible was born into
was very different from the one we know today.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.849
For one thing, it wasn’t really called Russia,
but Muscovy, or The Grand Duchy of Moscow

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:14.510
if you want to be old fashioned.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:15.849
For another, it was tiny.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:20.650
Comparative to modern Russia, it was nothing;
a landlocked strip hemmed in on all sides

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:21.810
by rival states.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:22.980
But it was something else, too.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.620
The Muscovy of 1530 was weak.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.970
The superpowers of the day were Poland-Lithuania
- which hadn’t technically yet become a

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.420
fully joint state, but was so close that we’re
just gonna say Poland-Lithuania to keep things

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.540
easy - and the Khanate of Crimea.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.730
Alongside them were a hodgepodge of weaker
Islamic Khanates leftover from Ghengis Khan’s

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.510
Golden Horde, Khanates like Kazan, and Siberia.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.709
Don’t worry about remembering all those
names, we’ll remind you of them when the

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:49.709
time comes.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.460
Just know that, at the moment of Ivan’s
birth, Muscovy was a tiny outpost of Orthodox

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:59.070
Christianity surrounded by a sea of Islam
and Catholicism.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.450
Not that this concerned young Ivan much.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.440
He was a baby, too busy playing to care about
affairs of state.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.340
That was what dad was there for.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.670
Yeah, about that.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:13.810
In 1533, Vasili III contracted an infection
which swiftly turned him from ‘strong king’

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.440
into ‘dying king’.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.250
Sensing the end was near, the Grand Prince
summoned the boyars, a class of warrior nobles

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.349
who held much of Muscovy’s power.

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:25.870
Vasili made them swear loyalty to his son
Ivan, before setting up a regency with his

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.030
second wife Elena ruling in Ivan’s name.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.090
That done, he promptly died.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.080
Shortly after, on December 4, 1533, little
Ivan was proclaimed Grand Prince of Muscovy.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.590
While this sounds like every spoiled brat’s
dream, the reality was less glamorous.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:46.270
From that point on, his mother Elena insisted
he be surrounded at all times by armed guards

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.690
in case the boyars tried to kill him and assume
the throne.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:53.049
But Elena needn’t have worried that the
boyars might kill little Ivan.

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:57.210
Why murder a child when it’s so much easier
to simply replace his regent?

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:02.580
On April 4, 1538, Elena died, almost certainly
assassinated by the boyars.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.950
Her death set off an explosive power struggle
in the Muscovite court.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.080
For Ivan, this meant spending his late childhood
trapped in a whirlwind of cruelty.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.720
The boyars knew no limits.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.750
Like warlords, they would do anything to keep
themselves in power.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:20.889
In one instance, Ivan witnessed a mentor being
skinned alive for crossing a powerful family.

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:22.400
It was also a time of neglect.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.990
Ivan would later recall that - despite living
in a palace - he wore only rags, and often

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.080
had to beg for food.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.289
The only time the boyars cared about the young
Grand Prince was when some foreign dignitary

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:33.289
visited.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:36.969
Then Ivan would be dressed up and paraded
about before being dumped back in his gilded

00:04:36.969 --> 00:04:37.969
prison.

00:04:37.969 --> 00:04:41.150
With all this cruelty around him, it’s perhaps
no surprise that Ivan began taking out his

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.520
anger on animals.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.830
By age 12, he was torturing cats, much as
he’d witnessed the boyars torturing their

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:47.830
rivals.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:52.159
But if the boyars thought they could cow this
weak and useless boy, they were about to learn

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:53.550
a terrible lesson.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.270
Ivan may have barely been a teenager.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.690
But already he was plotting his revenge.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:04.279
Building The Third Rome

00:05:04.279 --> 00:05:09.619
On December 29, 1543, a group of guards marched
into the quarters of one of the cruelest boyars

00:05:09.619 --> 00:05:10.819
in Muscovy.

00:05:10.819 --> 00:05:14.439
Saying they were following the Grand Prince’s
orders, the guards arrested the man, dragged

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:18.379
him away...and fed him alive to a pack of
wild dogs.

00:05:18.379 --> 00:05:22.740
It was the Middle Ages equivalent of walking
into the prison yard and punching the biggest,

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.360
meanest skinhead smack in the mouth.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.099
Aged only 13, Ivan had just ordered his first
gruesome murder.

00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:33.659
From that point on, the boyars would show
him respect.

00:05:33.659 --> 00:05:35.729
Not that Ivan did the same for others around
him.

00:05:35.729 --> 00:05:39.869
By 14, the newly-confidant Grand Prince was
wandering the streets of Moscow with his teenage

00:05:39.869 --> 00:05:43.589
friends, beating and robbing any citizen unlucky
enough to cross his path.

00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:46.289
Yet it would be unfair to say that Ivan was
merely a thug.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:51.009
At the same time he was getting a taste for
brutality, the boy was getting a taste for

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:52.360
something else, too.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.600
Ivan was discovering religion.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.869
Around the same time as his first murder,
Ivan had fallen under the sway of a Metropolitan

00:05:58.869 --> 00:06:04.149
known as Macarius, who had impressed upon
Ivan the need for Muscovy to become a bastion

00:06:04.149 --> 00:06:05.870
of Orthodox Christianity.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.649
And Macarius knew just how to do that.

00:06:08.649 --> 00:06:11.960
In 1547, Ivan’s regency finally came to
an end.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:17.550
On January 16 , he was crowned Tsar Ivan IV
of all Russians in a lavish ceremony overseen

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.909
by Macarius.

00:06:18.909 --> 00:06:20.960
This was a super big deal.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.860
Ivan was the first person to be crowned tsar.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:29.039
By using that title, and by using Byzantine
trappings in the coronation, Ivan and Macarius

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:33.979
were positioning Muscovy as the successor
state to the lost Byzantine Empire; the defender

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:35.119
of the Christian faith.

00:06:35.119 --> 00:06:37.929
They even began calling Moscow the Third Rome.

00:06:37.929 --> 00:06:41.520
Three weeks after this audacious ceremony,
Ivan held another one.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.029
On February 3, 1547, Tsar Ivan IV married
a young girl called Anastasia.

00:06:46.029 --> 00:06:50.119
You’ve probably never head of her, but you
have heard of her family.

00:06:50.119 --> 00:06:52.550
Anastasia was from the Romanovs.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:58.509
In the not-too distant future, her family
was going to transform Russia.

00:06:58.509 --> 00:07:03.169
But only after Ivan had had his turn.

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:07.710
No sooner was the Terrible on the throne than
he was firing off edicts, reshaping Muscovy

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.729
with far-reaching reforms.

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:12.259
Surprisingly, not all these reforms were terrible.

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:16.729
To go through all of them would take forever,
but you can sum them up as being largely about

00:07:16.729 --> 00:07:17.830
accountability.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.599
In the mid-sixteenth century, the aristocrats
could generally get away with just beating

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:24.830
you to death in the street because they felt
like it.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:29.139
Ivan’s new laws said “hey, actually, you
can’t just kill commoners for no reason.”

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:35.330
It may not sound like much but, coupled with
the introduction of localized government and

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.229
Muscovy’s first professional army, it was
a minor revolution.

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:42.159
Still, let’s not kid ourselves that Ivan
felt bound by his laws.

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:45.539
He’d designed them specifically to weaken
the power of the boyars.

00:07:45.539 --> 00:07:50.059
When a bunch of commoners came to him to complain
about a corrupt official, Ivan had all 70

00:07:50.059 --> 00:07:54.180
of them stripped naked in the snow before
setting their beards on fire.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.150
Still, by the time his fifth anniversary on
the throne rolled around, Ivan was in a pretty

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:59.219
good place domestically.

00:07:59.219 --> 00:08:04.139
He’d consolidated power, won the public’s
approval, and weakened the hated boyars.

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:09.860
Now he just needed his shiny new state to
find a way of living up to that whole Third

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:10.860
Rome thing.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.619
And what better way to do that than to launch
a crusade?

00:08:14.619 --> 00:08:19.830
The Fires of Victory

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.969
OK, time to check back in on all those neighbors
we mentioned earlier.

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:24.969
Remember?

00:08:24.969 --> 00:08:27.149
The ones leftover from Genghis Khan’s Golden
Horde?

00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:31.679
Well, the last few years had seen the Khanate
in Crimea growing bolder.

00:08:31.679 --> 00:08:35.169
Tartar armies were starting to harry the fringes
of Muscovy.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:38.019
But Crimea was far too strong for Ivan to
deal with now.

00:08:38.019 --> 00:08:43.740
No, he and Macarius had their sights set on
somewhere a little closer to home: Kazan.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.500
A minor Khanate sat on the Volga River, Kazan
was technically a threat to Muscovy, but was

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.269
actually a political basketcase.

00:08:50.269 --> 00:08:52.270
That’s not to say they were helpless.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:57.240
Over the years, many Muscovite armies had
tried to conquer Kazan, and just as many had

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.240
failed.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.720
Now he was secure in his position, Ivan was
determined to show everyone how it was done.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:08.040
On June 16, 1552, Ivan IV rode out at the
head of a vast army.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.420
Since this was his first major war, it was
imperative that everything went well.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:15.940
To that end, Ivan put aside hours per day
for praying, begging the Christian god to

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.870
help him crush the Muslim infidels.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.450
It seemed God was listening.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.680
On September 2, Ivan’s armies laid siege
to Kazan.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.079
They cut off supplies, destroyed access to
drinking water, and built huge earthen works

00:09:28.079 --> 00:09:30.259
to defend from cavalry attack.

00:09:30.259 --> 00:09:32.240
By early October, it was over.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:37.149
Kazan fell on October 13, just two days after
Ivan’s son Dimitri was born.

00:09:37.149 --> 00:09:40.360
Although the Khanate tried to return, it was
effectively dead.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.519
Ivan had done it.

00:09:41.519 --> 00:09:44.360
Not just done it, but done it with astounding
ease.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.589
Had he died now, it’s likely that Ivan would’ve
gone down in history as a great ‘What if?’,

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:54.770
a ruler history buffs singled out as someone
with the potential to be a legend.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:59.230
This is worth thinking about, because, in
1553, Ivan really did nearly die.

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:01.230
That year, Ivan fell deathly ill.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:05.949
Thinking it was the end, he did as his father,
Vasili III, had done, and summoned the boyars

00:10:05.949 --> 00:10:08.690
to swear an oath to his son Dimitri.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:10.980
This time, the boyars refused.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:12.759
They prefered Ivan’s cousin.

00:10:12.759 --> 00:10:16.940
If Ivan really was dying, he was just gonna
have to deal with this being the end of his

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:18.019
royal line.

00:10:18.019 --> 00:10:19.939
However, Ivan didn’t die.

00:10:19.939 --> 00:10:23.060
He recovered, but came back slightly changed.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:27.769
Always a paranoid man, Ivan felt like his
illness had confirmed his darkest fears.

00:10:27.769 --> 00:10:30.000
The boyars really were out to get him.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.850
At that moment, Ivan vowed to get them first.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:38.040
The Jaws of Defeat

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:44.319
Come winter, 1557, Ivan was looking for an
excuse to expand Russia’s boarders.

00:10:44.319 --> 00:10:49.610
The year before, his armies had annexed the
other Khanate on the Volga, Astrakhan, giving

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.300
Muscovy clear access to the Caspian Sea.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:56.389
But all the victory had done had convinced
Ivan that his new Russian state needed access

00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:59.899
to an actual sea, one that connected to real
oceans.

00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:01.939
One like the Baltic Sea.

00:11:01.939 --> 00:11:06.680
In those days, Muscovy was bordered by something
known as Livonia, a state roughly analogous

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.329
to modern day Latvia and Estonia.

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:14.120
While Kazan had been a remnant of the Golden
Horde, Livonia was a remnant of the land the

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.639
Teutonic Knights had conquered.

00:11:15.639 --> 00:11:21.019
But with the Knights now gone and Protestantism
on the rise, Livonia’s Catholic rulers were

00:11:21.019 --> 00:11:22.279
starting to look weak.

00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:28.459
Then, in December, 1557, King Sigismund of
Poland-Lithuania inserted himself into Livonia’s

00:11:28.459 --> 00:11:31.300
affairs, forcing the nation to sign a treaty
with him.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:35.339
For Ivan, this flagrant provocation was the
excuse he’d been looking for.

00:11:35.339 --> 00:11:40.250
The moment the atrocious winter weather cleared
up, Ivan was leading a full-on invasion force

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:44.430
into Livonia, determined to carve a path to
the Baltic coast.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:49.290
To say the war started well is to miss just
how easy a victory this was.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:52.769
The new Muscovy armies steamrollered the Livonians.

00:11:52.769 --> 00:11:57.720
By 1560, the Livonian Order was in ruins,
and its Grand Master had been dragged off

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:59.100
to Moscow and executed.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:02.139
It should’ve been another easy win for Ivan.

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:04.180
Unfortunately, it was anything but.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:08.019
In 1561, a new Grand Master was appointed
for Livonia.

00:12:08.019 --> 00:12:11.949
With commendable slyness, he surrendered not
to Ivan, but to Sigismund, thereby giving

00:12:11.949 --> 00:12:15.420
Poland-Lithuania a claim to the territory
Russia had occupied.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:20.070
At the same time, the Grand Master ditched
his Catholicism and invited the Protestant

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.079
Swedes to invade and restore order in the
north.

00:12:23.079 --> 00:12:28.079
And, just like that, Ivan found himself suddenly
stuck knee-deep in a war that would last the

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:29.149
next twenty years.

00:12:29.149 --> 00:12:31.730
It couldn’t have come at a worse time.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:36.540
In 1561, Ivan was still reeling from the double
whammy of his son Dimitri’s death, and the

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.350
suspected assassination of his wife, Anastasia.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:43.660
Although Ivan still had two heirs - his sons
Ivan the younger and the mentally challenged

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:48.160
Fyodor - the sudden loss of two family members
still left him bitter.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:53.990
When Metropolitan Macarius too passed away
in early 1563, Ivan seems to have lost it.

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.629
That same year, he beat one of his own soldiers
to death - the first murder the tsar committed

00:12:57.629 --> 00:12:58.820
with his own hands.

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:04.540
But it was the events of April 30, 1564, that
would really make Ivan into ‘the Terrible’.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:09.910
That day, Ivan’s best general, Andrey Kurbsky,
defected to the Lithuanians.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:11.459
This seems to have floored Ivan.

00:13:11.459 --> 00:13:14.990
Raging that the boyars were making a fool
of him, he announced he would abdicate the

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:15.990
throne.

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:18.209
You might expect the boyars to jump for joy
at this.

00:13:18.209 --> 00:13:22.019
After all, this was the guy who’d had one
of their number thrown to wild dogs.

00:13:22.019 --> 00:13:23.019
But this was war.

00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:27.490
If the tsar left now, the power vacuum it
created would potentially cause Russia to

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:28.490
implode.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:29.589
The boyars had no choice.

00:13:29.589 --> 00:13:31.330
They begged Ivan to stay.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:36.199
In early 1565, Ivan finally agreed, on one
condition: that he be allowed to create a

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:41.720
new mini-state within Russia itself, a place
where he would rule as God, without any checks

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:42.850
or balances.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.660
Backed into a corner, the boyars agreed.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:14:23.829
It was a decision that would set Muscovy on
the path

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:38.529
to Hell.

00:14:38.529 --> 00:14:39.889
The Oprichnina

00:14:39.889 --> 00:15:13.939
Shortly after, Ivan divided his 
nation into two parts: the Zemschina and the

00:15:13.939 --> 00:15:15.879
Oprichnina.

00:15:15.879 --> 00:15:39.420
The Zemschina was

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.430
where things carried on as before, almost
unchanged.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:46.899
The Oprichnina was where Ivan’s darkest
impulses came out to play.

00:15:46.899 --> 00:15:51.369
The Oprichnina covered about a third of Muscovy’s
territory, but it wasn’t as simple as drawing

00:15:51.369 --> 00:15:52.430
a line on a map.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:56.920
Ivan was allowed to choose what he wanted
in his new kingdom, so naturally he selected

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:02.279
the richest towns, meaning the borders between
Oprichnina and Zemschina were often blurred.

00:16:02.279 --> 00:16:06.970
In Moscow itself, the border could change
street by street, even house by house.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.860
You might never know when you were suddenly
subject to Ivan’s whims.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:15.079
And this was a problem, because it wasn’t
Ivan doing the Oprichnina’s dirty work.

00:16:15.079 --> 00:16:16.269
It was the Oprichniki.

00:16:16.269 --> 00:16:22.220
An army of around 6,000 men, the Oprichniki
were effectively Russia’s first secret police.

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:27.000
Dressed all in black, they rode black horses
and drove black carriages designed to instill

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.000
fear.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.089
They were completely above the law, given
the right to torture, rob, rape or kill anyone

00:16:31.089 --> 00:16:34.220
they felt like, and to burn down whatever
buildings they wished to.

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:38.440
Known as “children of darkness”, they
had one mission: to terrify the populace into

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:39.440
submission.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.350
You better believe they succeeded.

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:45.829
To relate every awful thing the Oprichniki
did would result in this video being 10,000

00:16:45.829 --> 00:16:48.660
hours long and giving all of us PTSD.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:53.399
So let’s just stick to examples, like the
palace they used for their headquarters, where

00:16:53.399 --> 00:16:56.509
it’s said twenty people were tortured to
death every single day.

00:16:56.509 --> 00:17:01.440
Or like their sadistic commander, Malyuta
Skuratov, who liked to round up married women

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:04.860
from across Moscow and have them all raped
for his entertainment.

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:09.159
With Ivan’s blessing the Oprichniki targeted
boyar families, murdering or driving them

00:17:09.159 --> 00:17:11.990
into exile, seizing the properties they left
behind.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:16.419
By the end of the 1560s, they had turned so
many wealthy families into refugees that the

00:17:16.419 --> 00:17:20.150
Oprichniki had managed to set themselves up
as a new aristocracy.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.459
But where was Ivan in all this?

00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:23.459
Leading from the front.

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:28.559
In early 1570, for example, Ivan became convinced
that the city of Novgorod was about to join

00:17:28.559 --> 00:17:29.740
the Lithuanians.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:31.250
There was no evidence for this.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:35.940
In fact, Ivan had to make his rapist-in-chief
Malyuta Skuratov forge documents to “prove”

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:38.159
Novgorod was on the verge of rebelling.

00:17:38.159 --> 00:17:41.970
Nevertheless, on January 2, Ivan rode out
at the head of a vast Oprichniki army.

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:47.409
For the next month, the Oprichniki were allowed
free run of Novgorod, killing, burning, maiming

00:17:47.409 --> 00:17:51.200
like some demented, sixteenth century version
of The Purge.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.210
Citizens were hanged by the hundreds.

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:55.960
Others were tied up and dumped into the ice-covered
river.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:59.480
Those that managed to get back to the surface
had their heads caved in with axes.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:04.700
By the time the Oprichniki left Novgorod on
February 12, there was no Novgorod left.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:09.410
In one short month, the Oprichniki had killed
anywhere between 6,000 and 12,000 people.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:14.190
Back in Moscow, Ivan followed this brutal,
pointless act by accusing boyars of conspiring

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:16.650
with Novgorod and having them executed.

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:19.150
By now, there was a feeling like nobody in
Muscovy was safe.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:21.170
That this horror would never end.

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:25.679
However, help was about to come from an unlikely
source.

00:18:25.679 --> 00:18:30.809
War is Over

00:18:30.809 --> 00:18:34.350
The Oprichnina’s end came suddenly, in spring,
1571.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:39.669
There were rumors that a Crimean Tartar force
was marching toward Moscow, but Ivan barely

00:18:39.669 --> 00:18:40.900
bothered to prepare.

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:45.980
After all, his Oprichniki spies had told him
the force was a few thousand strong at best.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:50.020
In fact, the advancing army contained over
120,000 soldiers.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:54.140
By the time Ivan got the news, any chance
to fortify the city was gone.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:59.669
So Ivan simply grabbed as much treasure as
he could and fled, leaving Moscow to its fate.

00:18:59.669 --> 00:19:02.230
And what an awful fate it was.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:04.340
The Tartars ransacked the city.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.240
Muscovites were dragged away into slavery.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.450
Children were slaughtered in their homes.

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:12.080
Every single building but the Kremlin was
burned to the ground.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:17.559
By the time the Tartar force retreated, on
May 26, over 160,000 Muscovites were dead

00:19:17.559 --> 00:19:18.770
or in chains.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:22.980
So many bodies had been dumped in the river
that it had actually changed course.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.470
The next year, Ivan abolished the Oprichnina.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:29.460
Perhaps to cover his own failings, he also
unleashed a new terror against the Oprichniki

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:33.049
for failing to protect Moscow, paying back
blood with blood.

00:19:33.049 --> 00:19:34.520
But it was little use.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:39.020
Russia was by now a war-shattered ruin, with
an exhausted populace and an economy on the

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:40.380
brink of collapse.

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:44.590
Perhaps the only silver lining was that the
Crimean Tartar army finally overextended itself

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:47.779
shortly after and was crushed by regular Russian
forces.

00:19:47.779 --> 00:19:50.470
Still, this offered only limited respite.

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:54.940
If Ivan were to save his nation, there was
only one thing he could do.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:57.570
Ivan would have to end the Livonian War.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:58.570
But he didn’t.

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:03.029
Although it was by now clear that his boneheaded
invasion of the Baltics had caused these endless

00:20:03.029 --> 00:20:06.990
crises, Ivan still couldn’t give up his
old ways.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:10.880
In the early 1570s, he even tried instituting
a new reign of terror, having his opponents

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:12.740
at home roasted alive.

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:16.510
But his real enemy was the senseless paranoia
in his own mind.

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:21.760
While Ivan was busy killing boyars, Poland-Lithuania’s
new king was invading Russia, and the Swedes

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.870
were reconquering Russia’s gains in Livonia.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.250
Yet Ivan refused to listen to reason.

00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:32.059
No matter what happened, he failed to see
that he himself was at fault.

00:20:32.059 --> 00:20:36.549
However, while Ivan the Terrible was having
a truly terrible time, his son Ivan was doing

00:20:36.549 --> 00:20:37.630
rather better.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:42.510
A capable general, Ivan the younger had fought
the Polish-Lithuanian army in battle and acquitted

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:43.510
himself well.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:46.540
So well, in fact, that the boyars wrote to
Tsar Ivan saying “hey, doofus.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:49.620
Why not have this guy be leader, huh?”

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:50.900
They meant leader of the army.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:53.940
But Ivan the Terrible thought they meant to
make his son tsar.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.010
It was a suggestion he didn’t take kindly.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:21:01.710
In November, 1581, shortly after receiving
the boyars’ petition, Ivan stumbled across

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:03.540
his son’s pregnant wife.

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:07.200
In a rage, he beat her so savagely that she
miscarried.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.810
When his son came to complain, the old monster
grabbed his staff and hit the boy around the

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:12.810
head with it.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:17.670
He kept right on hitting him until his skull
had broken and the floor was slick with blood.

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:21.050
Ivan the younger died of his injuries on November
19, 1581.

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:26.809
It’s said that Tsar Ivan reacted to his
passing by wailing “my God, what have I

00:21:26.809 --> 00:21:27.809
done?”

00:21:27.809 --> 00:21:30.220
But it was even worse than the puffed-up sadist
realized.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:34.529
With Ivan the younger as dead as Dimitri and
Anastasia, the last heir to the throne was

00:21:34.529 --> 00:21:37.090
Ivan’s simple-minded son Fyodor.

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:41.630
In just a few short years, Fyodor’s ascension
to the throne was going to both plunge Russia

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:47.659
into chaos, and end Ivan the Terrible’s
family line for good.

00:21:47.659 --> 00:21:52.600
The Price of Peace

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:57.010
If nothing else, the death of Ivan the younger
seems to have finally focused Ivan’s crazed

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:58.010
mind.

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:01.840
In 1581, the Tsar of all Russians formally
asked the Pope to negotiate an end to his

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:03.570
war with Poland-Lithuania.

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:07.490
The eventual peace process saw Russia surrender
all its gains in Livonia.

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:12.419
A separate peace with Sweden saw Russia abandon
its claims to coastal towns on the gulf of

00:22:12.419 --> 00:22:13.419
Finland.

00:22:13.419 --> 00:22:17.760
After twenty four years of war… after the
deaths of hundreds of thousands of people…

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:21.039
Russia was right back where it had been in
1558.

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:22.620
All that horror.

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:23.740
All that suffering.

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:25.760
The Oprichnina, all of it.

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:28.070
All had been for nothing.

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:33.169
The only silver lining had been the destruction
of the Khanates in the early days of Ivan’s

00:22:33.169 --> 00:22:37.360
reign, and the implosion of the Crimean army
after it sacked Moscow.

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:42.130
And the second of those had been in spite
of Ivan, rather than because of him.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:46.279
Interestingly enough, Ivan scored one other
great victory in the last days of his reign

00:22:46.279 --> 00:22:48.899
that had absolutely nothing to do with him.

00:22:48.899 --> 00:22:54.090
In 1583, the Stroganoff family - yep, just
like the dish - rode a private army into Siberia

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:57.760
almost on a whim and defeated the Khanate
there.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:02.380
This simple victory would open up Russian
expansion into Siberia for centuries to come,

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:07.220
providing a wealth of riches that would help
bring the nation back to its former glory.

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:10.330
So, um, well done Mr. Stroganoff.

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:14.700
By 1584, Ivan was so ill that even leaving
bed was too much for him.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:20.500
His only solace was playing chess, something
he was doing on March 18 when a stroke allegedly

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:21.740
carried him off.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:26.909
We say “allegedly” because it’s long
been rumored that the tyrant tsar was simply

00:23:26.909 --> 00:23:29.330
strangled by fed-up boyars.

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:33.460
In the aftermath of Ivan’s death, Fyodor
took the throne, but was so useless that his

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:38.480
rule led to something called the Time of Troubles,
a fifteen year period of civil wars and uprisings

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:42.750
that made the Oprichnina look like Disneyland
constructed from unicorns.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:47.519
In fact, the chaos Ivan the Terrible left
in his wake only cleared when Anastasia’s

00:23:47.519 --> 00:23:51.460
old family, the Romanovs, finally seized power
in 1613.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:56.890
At the end of all that, then, what can we
say about the man known to history as “the

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:57.890
Terrible”?

00:23:57.890 --> 00:24:00.250
Well, he certainly lived up to his nickname!

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:05.090
Seriously, next time someone bangs on about
how “Terrible” is a mistranslation of

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:10.490
Grozny, just remember that, mistranslation
or not, “Terrible” is certainly appropriate.

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:14.320
But there are darker lessons we can take from
Ivan’s story.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:18.760
The idea of an unaccountable leader who ruled
through fear, and of a secret police run by

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:24.029
sadists with no checks on their power would
return 500 years later, under perhaps the

00:24:24.029 --> 00:24:26.870
only Russian leader more terrible than Ivan.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:30.240
It’s said that Josef Stalin admired Ivan
the Terrible.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:34.760
Maybe he felt a kindred spirit, calling to
him down through the years, encouraging him

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:40.090
to go even further, to commit even worse crimes
than had happened during the Oprichnina.

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:43.080
It’s often said that history repeats itself.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:47.539
Well, even the briefest look at Russian history
will show you that Ivan’s tale repeated

00:24:47.539 --> 00:24:52.919
itself again and again, like a record needle
stuck in a groove.

00:24:52.919 --> 00:25:01.480
Ivan may have been the first crazed autocrat
to terrorize Russia.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:23.860
But, he wouldn’t be the last.

